{
  "id": "bev-car",
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "name": "Battery Electric Car",
  "description": "Compact electric car modelled on the 2021 Cupra Born 58 kWh (VW ID.3 class), with the values of FASTSim's vehicle file (Apache-2.0): 1,927 kg, Cd 0.27, 2.31 m², 150 kW e-motor, 62 kWh battery with 58 kWh usable, 0.25 kW auxiliaries. HV battery → inverter → e-motor → single-speed reduction gear → wheels, driven over a speed profile.\nWhat to expect (energy at the battery terminals):\n• WLTC Class 3b: 14.1 kWh/100 km (a car of this class is rated about 15–16 kWh/100 km at the charging socket, charging losses included)\n• WLTC with heating/air-con on (2.5 kW): 18.9 kWh/100 km\n• City Cycle: 11.1 kWh/100 km\n• Full power: 0–100 km/h in 7.1 s, top speed 160 km/h (Cupra: 7.3 s, 160 km/h)",
  "card": {
    "question": "How much energy does a compact electric car use in the city, on the WLTC, and with the heating on?",
    "tags": [
      "battery electric",
      "energy use",
      "drive cycles",
      "WLTC"
    ],
    "difficulty": "beginner",
    "runTimeS": 10,
    "learn": [
      "Read the energy per 100 km and the battery's state of charge after a cycle",
      "See what an auxiliary load such as heating costs on a cycle",
      "Compare two runs of one car"
    ],
    "status": "plausibility-checked",
    "features": [
      "Driving Task with a bundled drive cycle",
      "Battery, E-Motor, single-speed reduction gear",
      "Auxiliary load (Power Consumer)",
      "Live run at 10× real time"
    ],
    "author": "LightSim",
    "version": "0.3.0",
    "licence": "LightSim's own (LICENSE); vehicle values from FASTSim (Apache-2.0), see the data register DR-01",
    "narrative": [
      "0 s: the car starts at 90 % charge, standing.",
      "On the City Cycle it accelerates to 50 km/h, cruises and stops twice; braking recuperates energy into the battery.",
      "On the WLTC it drives four phases, Low, Medium, High and Extra High, up to 131 km/h; the air drag of the Extra High phase uses the most energy.",
      "With the heating/air-con on, the 2.5 kW load runs the whole 1,800 s and adds about 4.8 kWh/100 km."
    ]
  },
